    "q-161614": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161614",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ for $y\\le 0,$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14247307/assets/350b2dc98a09957c17f1ab629e21021c30fcccd9443068c722fe7565f04dcbfe.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9={r}^{2}+9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{r}^{2}+9}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-161614-a",
